# MoE host CPU compute tier: kernel, mmap weight accessor, counters, property
#
# M14 steals the (token,expert) pairs that would otherwise force an eviction
# in M9's device slot pool and computes them on the host CPU instead of
# uploading them over PCIe. This patch is the compute half only: the kernel,
# its host-only red-first test, the mmap weight accessor, the M14 perf
# counters, the two new plugin properties, and the AVX2 build wiring. Nothing
# in it changes served behaviour — MOE_CPU_TIER exists as a property and does
# nothing until patches/0012 (the decode-path redirect and the kernel
# sentinel) wires it up. Applied against the pinned runtime, on top of
# patches/0001-0010.
#
# 1. Correction to the inputs (o-m14-design.md §2 and the task brief both say
#    "weights source is host-addressable; slot buffers usm_host" -- wrong).
#    moe_otd::fill_weights_memory (moe_otd_runtime.cpp) reads each tensor with
#    weight_reader.read(...) -> ov::util::positional_read on a plain file
#    handle (moe_otd_runtime.hpp's ParallelWeightReader), into a loop-local
#    std::vector<uint8_t> payload. There is no mmap, no usm_host mirror of
#    the expert weights anywhere in the plugin: the usm_host buffers that do
#    exist are the async-upload staging ring (patches/0006) and the slot
#    buffers themselves (device- or host-tier per patches/0005) -- both are
#    upload *destinations*, never a host-readable source. Consequence:
#    ParallelWeightReader gained a new mapped(offset, size) accessor
#    (ov::load_mmap_object on the same file positional_read() already opens,
#    so the two views cannot disagree), lazy so a tier-off run never creates
#    the mapping. Reusing the existing payload vector was rejected: it would
#    cost a fresh 1.6 MiB pread + memcpy per expert per token on the decode
#    critical path, exactly the cost this milestone exists to avoid paying
#    for a capacity miss.
#
# 2. The weight nibble order, derived from the generated kernel, not assumed.
#    maybe_transpose_scale_zp returns immediately for offset_pos 0-2 (gate/up/
#    down weights) -- file layout equals device layout for the raw weight
#    bytes, only the scale/zp tensors get transposed on upload
#    (moe_otd_runtime.cpp). The GEMV reads column n at B = weight + n*K/2
#    (moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl:105, 656), K = input dim. Within a column, byte
#    j holds element 2j in the LOW nibble and element 2j+1 in the HIGH
#    nibble -- and that pairing has to be *derived*, because the GEMV's lane
#    pattern looks half-block interleaved and is not:
#
#    At mlp.cl:547-551 (identically at 1058-1062), the kernel de-interleaves
#    x into a staging buffer x2 before the dot product ever runs: for each
#    FAKE_GROUP block, `px2[j] = px[2*j]` and `px2[j + FAKE_GROUP_SIZE/2] =
#    px[2*j+1]` -- i.e. x2's low half holds x's even elements, x2's high half
#    holds x's odd elements, both still in original block-position order j.
#    In the ELEMS_PER_LANE==4 branch (mlp.cl:126-133, the served
#    SUBGROUP_SIZE=32 card's path), lane L reads `a.s0 = x2[gk*FG + L]`, `a.s2 = x2[gk*FG +
#    L + 2*SG] = x2[gk*FG + FG/2 + L]`, and pairs them with
#    `DEQUANT_4BIT_LO(b.s0)` and `DEQUANT_4BIT_HI(b.s0)` where
#    `b.s0 = B[gk*FG/2 + L]`. Substituting the staging map back:
#    `a.s0 = x2[gk*FG + L] = x[gk*FG + 2*L]` (L < FG/2, so this reads x2's
#    "even" half) and `a.s2 = x2[gk*FG + FG/2 + L] = x[gk*FG + 2*L + 1]`
#    (this reads x2's "odd" half). So LO pairs with x[gk*FG + 2*L], HI pairs
#    with x[gk*FG + 2*L + 1], and the byte doing the pairing sits at block
#    position L -- i.e. byte j (relative to the group) holds element 2j (low
#    nibble) and element 2j+1 (high nibble), exactly the "standard packing"
#    stated above. The same substitution on `b.s1` (block position L+SG)
#    yields elements 2*(L+SG) and 2*(L+SG)+1, consistent. Identical result,
#    same substitution, in the ELEMS_PER_LANE==2 branch (mlp.cl:151-159, the
#    SUBGROUP_SIZE=16 card's path: b is a single byte at block position L, a.s0/a.s1
#    read x2 at L and SG+L, i.e. x[2L] and x[2L+1]) and the ELEMS_PER_LANE==8
#    branch (mlp.cl:171-193). The interleave is a GPU staging trick that
#    exists to make block reads coalesced; it has no bearing on what is
#    actually stored in the weight file. A reader who sees the "half-block
#    interleaved" pairing in the GEMV's a/b index arithmetic and stops there,
#    without following it through the x2 staging step, will conclude the
#    opposite (byte j holds elements j and j+K/2) and get every dot product
#    wrong by a data-dependent permutation -- this is exactly T1's red case 1
#    (see the test file and this patch's report for the verbatim red run).
#
#    Sign extension iff the weight dtype is i4 (WEIGHT_IS_SIGNED,
#    moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp:428; 0 on the served u4 artifact):
#    q = (v ^ 8) - 8. Unsigned: q = v.
#
#    Scales (offset_pos 3-5): device layout is [group][oc] (the GEMV reads
#    S = scales + n; s0 = S[g*N]). The FILE layout is the transpose,
#    [oc][group], because maybe_transpose_scale_zp maps
#    src_elem_idx = o*group_count + g -> dst_elem_idx = g*oc + o
#    (moe_otd_runtime.cpp). The CPU kernel reads the file layout and must NOT
#    transpose: scale[n][g] is the f16 at linear index n*group_count + g --
#    which also happens to be the better layout for a kernel that walks one
#    output column at a time.
#
#    Zero points (offset_pos 6-8, u4): device layout is [group][oc/2], low
#    nibble = even n. File layout: element (o,g) at linear index
#    o*group_count + g, packed two per byte with the even linear index in
#    the low nibble (moe_otd_runtime.cpp). So: idx = n*group_count + g;
#    byte = zp_base[idx>>1]; z = (idx&1) ? (byte>>4) : (byte&0x0F), never
#    sign-extended. group_count is 32 (gate/up) and 8 (down) on the served
#    artifact -- both even, so within a column the pairs happen to line up
#    as (g=0,1),(2,3),... but the kernel does not rely on that, only on the
#    formula, since a future model with an odd group_count would break that
#    coincidence.
#
# 5. Rounding, matched stage-by-stage to the three real stores the GPU makes
#    (moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl), because the CPU kernel is explicitly NOT
#    bit-equal to the GPU -- G1b (patches/0012's equivalence gate) is a
#    tolerance gate, not byte-exactness, for exactly this reason:
#
#      up_h[j] = f16( acc_up[j] )                        -- mlp.cl store, up's y[n] = sum_all1
#      h[j]    = f16( up_h[j] * act_f32(acc_gate[j]) )    -- mlp.cl store, gate's y[n] *= ACT(sum_all0)
#      y[n]    = f16( acc_down[n] * f32(w_route) )        -- mlp.cl:758-759
#
#    acc_up/acc_gate/acc_down are each f32-accumulated across every quant
#    group of their stage (a straightforward sum, no rounding surprises
#    there); the three lines above are the only three places a value crosses
#    an f16 boundary in the reference kernel (moe_cpu_expert_ref), and each
#    one is a deliberate, matched reproduction of a real GPU store, not an
#    incidental narrowing this patch introduced. act_f32 is SiLU
#    (x/(1+exp(-x))) by default, or the GeGLU-tanh / GeGLU-erf forms
#    transcribed verbatim from mlp.cl -- including the moe_mlp_fast_erf A&S
#    7.1.26 coefficients, not std::erf, since a library's erf is not
#    guaranteed to agree with the kernel's polynomial approximation to
#    anywhere near the precision this comparison needs.
#
#    What the CPU kernel deliberately does NOT reproduce: the GPU accumulates
#    each lane's within-fake-group partial products in `half` (mlp.cl:124-146,
#    the sum0/sum1 locals) before scaling and zp-correcting in float; the CPU
#    reference (moe_cpu_expert_ref) accumulates the whole dot product in f32
#    throughout. On the served config the half-accumulation chain is only 2
#    sequential FMAs deep (ELEMS_PER_LANE=2 on the SUBGROUP_SIZE=32 card, or 2 FMAs per
#    of 2 accumulators on the SUBGROUP_SIZE=16 card's ELEMS_PER_LANE=4), so the
#    divergence this introduces is small -- but it is not zero, and it is why
#    G1b (patches/0012) is a tolerance gate rather than a byte-exact one.
#    moe_cpu_expert_ref_gpu_emul (this patch's T1c) measures it host-only,
#    before any card window: it reproduces the same half-accumulation chain
#    structure (per lane: elems_per_lane/2 independent 2-deep half-FMA
#    chains, horizontally summed in float, matching every ELEMS_PER_LANE
#    branch in mlp.cl) rather than the exact SIMD lane-to-K-index mapping,
#    which is a scheduling detail with no numerical effect on a sum -- only
#    on its rounding path, which is the one thing being measured.
#
# Not covered by this patch (owed to patches/0012 or to the closing
# measurement): the decode-path redirect, the kernel sentinel skip and its
# barrier-uniformity argument, the policy decision on which miss goes to the
# CPU, and Ground rule 3's ik_llama.cpp/llama.cpp hybrid-offload survey.
#
# Deviation from the design sketch worth flagging on the record: §5.5 of the
# binding spec sketches an AVX2 kernel that processes NCOL=4 output columns
# per pass over the de-interleaved x vectors, to amortise x reuse across
# weight streams. moe_cpu_expert_avx2.cpp implements NCOL=1 instead --
# genuinely AVX2/FMA3/F16C-vectorised over the 64-element K-dimension chunks
# that are this kernel's actual correctness surface (the nibble order T1
# tests), but re-reading x once per column rather than once per four. Flagged
# as a throughput deviation, not a correctness one: a column-blocked rewrite
# is worth doing if and when T4's `avg_cpu_join_wait_us` says the AVX2 loop
# itself, not the mmap read or the join, is the binding cost.
#
#
# Integration note (orchestrator, after the implementer's hand-off): the
# [OTD_PERF] dump in moe_otd_runtime.cpp went through GPU_DEBUG_COUT, which
# compiles to `if (0)` in a plugin built without ENABLE_DEBUG_CAPS -- i.e.
# every packaged build. The counters are opt-in via MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG, so the
# dump now goes to std::cerr unconditionally when that opt-in is set; the
# GPU_DEBUG_* channels remain for the trace lines that are meant to be
# debug-only.
#
# Integration notes (orchestrator, after the first tier-ON windows):
#  - The NCOL=1 deviation above was right about the throughput and wrong
#    about the reason it mattered. The rewrite in moe_cpu_expert_avx2.cpp
#    de-interleaves the activations once per stage (xe/xo for the even/odd
#    nibbles, permuted to match a pshufb byte spread), keeps four
#    accumulators per column so the FMA chains overlap, and defers the
#    horizontal sum to the end of the column. NCOL stays 1: with four
#    accumulators a second column's state spills the 16 ymm registers
#    (measured on the assembly: 67 stack spills at NCOL=2, none at NCOL=1).
#    Measured from RAM (256 rotating synthetic experts of the served 35B
#    shape, single thread, -O3): 520 -> 198 us/expert. A one-expert loop
#    sits in L3 and reports 189 us for either regime; use the rotating
#    bench for anything that is meant to be a RAM number.
#  - This TU was being compiled at -Os. The graph library's Release
#    COMMON_COMPILE_OPTIONS appends -Os after the global -O3, and the per-
#    source AVX2 flags did not override it. Measured with the plugin's flag
#    order: 2404 us/expert against 198 at -O3, and the in-situ worker
#    counter (avg_cpu_task_us=2542) matched the -Os number. CMakeLists.txt
#    now appends -O3 to that one source's COMPILE_OPTIONS, which the build
#    emits after the target's options. Every tier-ON number taken before
#    this fix was a -Os kernel.
#  - Two worker-side counters added to moe_otd_perf.hpp and the dump:
#    avg_cpu_task_lag_us (dispatch to task start, the pool's wake cost)
#    and avg_cpu_task_us (the kernel on its worker thread). They are what
#    located the -Os defect: lag 6 us, task 2542 us, so neither the pool
#    nor the readback was the term to chase.
#  - Review follow-ups: the pool queue is a FIFO that dispatch() never
#    resets (the vector+cursor form could drop a batch under a concurrent
#    second dispatcher and hang wait(); unreachable from the engine, which
#    serialises lane inference, but reachable for any other client); the
#    AVX2 TU is wholly behind OV_MOE_CPU_TIER_HAVE_AVX2, so a build without
#    ENABLE_AVX2 compiles it to nothing however the target collects sources;
#    the dead byte-widening helper is gone and the file comment describes
#    the kernel that runs. "The binding spec" / "the design note" cited in
#    this header are session documents not in the repository.
